ger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ellen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Sanger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9bd30d0-8235-5ade-a1f7-dd6486a85f09', 'word_count': 1322, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9bd30d0-8235-5ade-a1f7-dd6486a85f09'}, {'abstract': 'Mark Peterson, the photographer who worked on a story for The Magazine about the secretary of defense, describes how he captured the press-averse official.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/01/insider/jim-mattis-photograph.html', 'snippet': 'Mark Peterson, the photographer who worked on a story for The Magazine about the secretary of defense, describes how he captured the press-averse official.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Defense Secretary James Mattis declined to sit for a portrait for Sunday’s New York Times Magazine cover story, “Last Man Standing,” by Robert F. Worth. So to provide readers with an original image of Mr. Mattis, the magazine needed to photograph the secretary during his limited public appearances, which happen about once a week when he briefly walks down the Pentagon steps to welcome visiting dignitaries.For the assignment, the magazine enlisted the photographer Mark Peterson, a contributor to The Times for over 20 years, who is known for his skill at capturing candid shots that tell a full story. Mr. Peterson took the picture that would become Sunday’s cover of The Times Magazine on his second trip to the Pentagon for this assignment, as Mr. Mattis met with the defense minister of Estonia in early March. Mr. Peterson recently explained how he got the shot.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/pageoneplus/02sum-magcover/02sum-magcover-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 731, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/02/pageoneplus/02sum-magcover/02sum-magcover-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 731}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/pageoneplus/02sum-magcover/02sum-magcover-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 841, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/pageoneplus/02sum-magcover/02sum-magcover-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1682, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/pageoneplus/02sum-magcover/02sum-magcover-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/02/pageoneplus/02sum-magcover/02sum-magcover-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/pageoneplus/02sum-magcover/02sum-magcover-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How a Photographer Got a Rare Shot of James Mattis', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When a Subject Refuses to Pose', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mattis, James N', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-01T23:49:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Insider', 'section_name': 'Times Insider', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Van Syckle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': 'Van', 'lastname': 'Syckle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6df5ca66-91ab-5f8e-86cc-7a343b462426', 'word_count': 609, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6df5ca66-91ab-5f8e-86cc-7a343b462426'}, {'abstract': 'If we learn anything from the recent controversies surrounding the social media giant, it should be that America’s privacy laws need beefing up.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/01/opinion/facebook-lax-privacy-rules.html', 'snippet': 'If we learn anything from the recent controversies surrounding the social media giant, it should be that America’s privacy laws need beefing up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As recently as 2010, Mark Zuckerberg, the founder and chief executive of Facebook, believed that privacy was no longer a “social norm.” But over the past few weeks — and not a moment too soon — he and his colleagues have learned that privacy still matters to individuals and society.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/02mon1/02mon1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 367, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/02mon1/02mon1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 367}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/02mon1/02mon1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 626, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/02mon1/02mon1-