ent_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Climate', 'section_name': 'Climate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brad Plumer and Nadja Popovich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Plumer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Nadja', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Popovich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/3f7949bd-7465-5e8e-bdac-e653be13524b', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/3f7949bd-7465-5e8e-bdac-e653be13524b'}, {'abstract': 'As China’s Xi Jinping consolidates power, owners of Hong Kong bookstores trafficking in banned books find themselves playing a very dangerous game.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/03/magazine/the-case-of-hong-kongs-missing-booksellers.html', 'snippet': 'As China’s Xi Jinping consolidates power, owners of Hong Kong bookstores trafficking in banned books find themselves playing a very dangerous game.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the police officer didn’t laugh, Lam Wing-kee knew he was in trouble. In his two decades as owner and manager of Hong Kong’s Causeway Bay Books, Lam had honed a carefully nonchalant routine when caught smuggling books into mainland China: apologize, claim ignorance, offer a cigarette to the officers, crack a joke. For most of his career, the routine was foolproof.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '42', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/magazine/08mag-bookseller1-alpha/08mag-bookseller1-alpha-articleLarge-v6.jpg', 'height': 723, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/08/magazine/08mag-bookseller1-alpha/08mag-bookseller1-alpha-articleLarge-v6.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight'