 [{'firstname': 'Alex', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Palmer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60fcc3f0-f510-588c-acf3-0f8cfadb275b', 'word_count': 4465, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60fcc3f0-f510-588c-acf3-0f8cfadb275b'}, {'abstract': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on whether to pass on information about a deceptive ex-spouse, a possible insurance fraud and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/03/magazine/should-the-new-girlfriend-of-an-awful-ex-husband-be-warned.html', 'snippet': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on whether to pass on information about a deceptive ex-spouse, a possible insurance fraud and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My sister-in-law was married to a man who looked good on paper and who was very charming. She found out after they were married that he lied to her about his employment history and history of drug and alcohol abuse, as well as about his finances. He had a breakdown soon after their child was born and received a diagnosis for a serious mental illness. When he was unmedicated, he was violent toward her. Even on medication, he has shown no remorse about the destruction he caused. He admitted that he spent the year before meeting her rehearsing his “story” so he could meet someone like her. He can still seem charming and, to an outsider, like a functioning adult.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/magazine/08mag-ethicist/08mag-08ethicist-t_CA0-articleLarge.png', 'height': 1045, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/08/magazine/08mag-ethicist/08mag-08ethicist-t_CA0-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1045}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/magazine/08mag-ethicist/08mag-08ethicist-t_CA0-jumbo.png', 'height': 1024, 'width': 588, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/magazine/08mag-ethicist/08mag-08ethicist-t_CA0-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1310, 'width': 752, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/magazine/08mag-ethicist/08mag-08ethicist-t_CA0-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/08/magazine/08mag-ethicist/08mag-08ethicist-t_CA0-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/magazine/08mag-ethicist/08mag-08ethicist-t_CA0-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Should the New Girlfriend of an Awful Ex-Husband Be Warned?', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Should the New Girlfriend Of an Awful Ex-Husband Be Warned?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dating and Relationships', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insurance', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Workplace Environment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lying', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-03T09:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kwame Anthony Appiah', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kwame', 'middlename': 'Anthony', 'lastname': 'Appiah', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3eafab8-7863-5761-b165-f6ef1864673b', 'word_count': 1262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3eafab8-7863-5761-b165-f6ef1864673b'}, {'abstract': 'A selection of books published this week; plus, a peek at what our colleagues around the newsroom are reading.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/03/books/review/new-noteworthy-motoko-rich.html', 'snippet': 'A selection of books published this week; plus, a peek at what our colleagues around the newsroom are reading.', 'lead_paragraph': 'POST TRAUMATIC HOOD DISORDER By David Tomas Martinez. (Sarabande, paper, $15.95.) In his second collection, Martinez has fun with the high-low mash-up that characterizes so much poetry today — one poem here is called “Footnoting Biggie Lyrics Like ‘Why Christmas Missed Us’” — but he also includes tender love poems and searching personal reminiscences. THE BLACK BEAR INSIDE ME By Robin Becker. (Pittsburgh, paper, $15.95.) These lilting, observational poems often invoke music: ballroom dancing, a Beethoven quartet, Scottish songs. One, “Reading Music,” concludes, “I remember when at five the marks on / the page became a story.” CENZONTLE By Marcelo Hernandez Castillo. (BOA Editions, paper, $16.) The title of Castillo’s debut means “mockingbird,” and birds flit in and out of these poems, singing our world back at us. Castillo embraces an expansive ambiguity — of language, of gender, of nationality — that can sound celebratory and mournful at once. EYE LEVEL By Jenny Xie. (Graywolf, paper, $16.) “Funny, the way we come to understand a place by wanting to escape it,” Xie writes, and the poems in her crystalline debut (with titles including “Rootless,” “Borderless,” “Displacement”) are ever alert to the complex relationship between geography and the self. IS THAT THE SOUND OF A PIANO COMING FROM SEVERAL HOUSES DOWN? By Noah Eli Gordon. (Solid Objects, $20.) The prose poems that make up the bulk of this slim book, all of them called “The Problem,” resemble absurdist flash fiction, disrupting reality through juxtaposition and non sequitur.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/books/review/08newnoteworthy-0/08newnoteworthy-0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 305, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/08/books/review/08newnoteworthy-0/08newnoteworthy-0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 305}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/books/review/08newnoteworthy-0/08newnoteworthy-0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 521, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/books/review/08newnoteworthy-0/08newnoteworthy-0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 789, 'width': 1551, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/books/review/08newnoteworthy-0/08newnoteworthy-0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/08/books/review/08newnoteworthy-0/08newnoteworthy-0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/books/review/08newnoteworthy-0/08newnoteworthy-0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New & Noteworthy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New &amp; Noteworthy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-03T09:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/becea0e3-3eb5-5438-8f47-52c90cc3c232', 'word_count': 430, 'uri': 'nyt://article/becea0e3-3eb5-5438-8f47-52c90cc3c232'}, {'abstract': 'The pre-Incan ruins of Kuélap share similarities with their more famous cousin. But getting there can be a herculean effort.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/03/travel/kuelap-peru-ruins-52-places.html', 'snippet': 'The pre-Incan ruins of Kuélap share similarities with their more famous cousin. But getting there can be a herculean effort.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was standing in a cloud forest in northern Peru, 10,000 feet above sea level. Quiet cloaked the more than 400 cylindrical homes and ceremonial buildings that make up the most important settlement of the Chachapoyas, or “Cloud Warrior” culture. Farther out were limestone walls up to 65 feet high. Tropical greenery sprang through cracks in the stone, while red bromeliads clung to moss-covered trees. In every direction was a backdrop of patchwork farmland clinging to the sides of mountains, above the Utcubamba River Valley. ', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/travel/03PERU-promo/03PERU-promo-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/02/travel/03PERU-promo/03PERU-promo-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/travel/03PERU-promo/03PERU-promo-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/travel/03PERU-promo/03PERU-promo-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/travel/03PERU-promo/03PERU-promo-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/02/travel/03PERU-promo/03PERU-promo-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/travel/03PERU-promo/03PERU-promo-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The 52 Places Traveler: In Peru, a Still-Hidden Alternative to Machu Picchu', 'kicker': 'Place Seven', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Into the Ancient Land of the Cloud Warriors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Peru', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHAEOLOGY AND ANTHROPOLOGY', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-03T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jada Yuan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jada', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yuan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01908385-c606-59d1-9610-7acfab573ccf', 'word_count': 1144, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01908385-c606-59d1-9610-7acfab573ccf'}, {'abstract': 'Volunteers are needed for recovery efforts in the Caribbean. Resorts, cruise lines and other organizations are offering special programs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/03/travel/volunteer-vacations-caribbean.html', 'snippet': 'Volunteers are needed for recovery efforts in the Caribbean. Resorts, cruise lines and other organizations are offering special programs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last fall, Kelly Hepburn, a real estate broker in Denver, thought about traveling to the U.S. Virgin Islands, but then Hurricanes Irma and Maria blew in, devastating the destination. In March, Ms. Hepburn decided to go to St. John solo to volunteer, joining projects cleaning up trails in a national park, painting at a local school and assisting at an animal shelter.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/travel/08volunteer-2/08volunteer-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/08/travel/08volunteer-2/08volunteer-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/travel/08volunteer-2/08volunteer-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/travel/08volunteer-2/08volunteer-2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/travel/08volunteer-2/08volunteer-2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/08/travel/08volunteer-2/08volunteer-2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/travel/08volunteer-2/08volunteer-2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Travelers Can Help Hurricane-Damaged Islands ', 'kicker': 'Trending', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mixing Work With Pleasure on Hurricane-Damaged Islands', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Volunteers and Community Service', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Maria (2017)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-03T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Glusac', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glusac', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/35af2142-888a-5b7a-9747-c13467141d0f', 'word_count': 877, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35af2142-888a-5b7a-9747-c13467141d0f'}, {'abstract': 'A dystopian show that will make you feel better about your own life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/03/magazine/letter-of-recommendation-house-hunters.html', 'snippet': 'A dystopian show that will make you feel better about your own life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I cannot watch “Black Mirror” anymore. Its metaphors have grown stale, its winks are now klaxons and its messages — once distorted and eerie — now land with a thud. The same holds true for most so-called “prestige” television, from the violent, misogynist dystopia of “The Handmaid’s Tale” to the violent, misogynist dystopia of “Westworld.” They feel inevitable and manipulative, and watching them does nothing but intensify my already simmering anxieties — which I suspect is the point. So nowadays, when the kitchen is clean and the cats are fed and my brain is on its downward spiral toward sleep, all I want to do is sink into the couch and turn on “House Hunters,” a show about a specific kind of darkness I have complete control in avoiding. It provides all the thrill of a dystopian hellscape with none of the fear.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/magazine/08mag-lor/08mag-08lor-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 447, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/08/magazine/08mag-lor/08mag-08lor-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 447}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/magazine/08mag-lor/08mag-08lor-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 763, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/magazine/08mag-lor/08mag-08lor-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1526, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image