, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3fa9cca-5739-5533-b23e-891eb7523c4b', 'word_count': 682, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3fa9cca-5739-5533-b23e-891eb7523c4b'}, {'abstract': 'Selected by Terrance Hayes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/04/magazine/poem-i-want-to-be-stark-like.html', 'snippet': 'Selected by Terrance Hayes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maybe you will say this “Game of Thrones”-inspired poem is mere play. Play is very worthwhile. Play with the multiple meanings of a word like “stark,” for example. Play with the sounds bouncing around: “stark” to “stock,” “famish” to “fathered.” When the first-person “I” enters the poem, play acquires a wonderful intimacy. “I would meet you upon this” is a private, possibly playful, promise. The last line is a song. Selected by Terrance Hayes', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/magazine/08mag-poem/08mag-poem-articleLarge.png', 'height': 294, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/08/magazine/08mag-poem/08mag-poem-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 294}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/magazine/08mag-poem/08mag-poem-jumbo.png', 'height': 501, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/magazine/08mag-poem/08mag-poem-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1002, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/magazine/08mag-poem/08mag-08poem-t_C