: 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9817b8cd-ae52-5d4e-ab5d-b6051b08e41d', 'word_count': 898, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9817b8cd-ae52-5d4e-ab5d-b6051b08e41d'}, {'abstract': 'Kate Mara and Jason Clarke star in a sober reconstruction of a fatal car crash in the summer of 1969.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/04/movies/chappaquiddick-review.html', 'snippet': 'Kate Mara and Jason Clarke star in a sober reconstruction of a fatal car crash in the summer of 1969.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I admit that I approached “Chappaquiddick” with a measure of skepticism and a tremor of dread. The ongoing, morbid fascination with all things Kennedy is an aspect of American culture I find perplexing and somewhat dispiriting. Compulsive Kennedyism encourages our unfortunate habit of substituting mythology for history, of dissolving the complexities of American political life into airy evocations of idealism and tragedy. The lives and deaths of Kennedy family members provide grist for endless conspiracy theorizing, tabloid salaciousness, celebrity-worship and superstition. A movie about one of the sadder, tawdrier episodes in the saga could only feed this syndrome.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/06/arts/06chappaquiddick/merlin_136310412_0b742f3a-6316-403b-9aca-d8e75c05d8b2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/06/arts/06chappaquiddick/merlin_136310412_0b742f3a-6316-403b-9aca-d8e75c05d8b2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/06/arts/06chappaquiddick/merlin_136310412_0b742f3a-6316-403b-9aca-d8e75c05d8b2-jumbo.jpg', 'h