, 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-04T23:50:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pat Borzi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pat', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Borzi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c614147-16b2-530c-a863-6a70d3f52d4d', 'word_count': 1041, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c614147-16b2-530c-a863-6a70d3f52d4d'}, {'abstract': 'People awaiting deportation are being forced to work for little or no pay. We have a name for that.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/04/opinion/migrants-detention-forced-labor.html', 'snippet': 'People awaiting deportation are being forced to work for little or no pay. We have a name for that.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We’re familiar with grim stories about black-shirted federal agents barging into apartment complexes, convenience stores and school pickup sites to round up and deport immigrants. We’ve heard far less about the forced labor — some call it slavery — inside detention facilities. But new legal challenges to these practices are succeeding and may stymie the government’s deportation agenda by taking profits out of the detention business.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/05/opinion/05stevens/merlin_136396896_4e098c8d-0660-41ba-9846-165586eca430-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/05/opinion/05stevens/merlin_136396896_4e098c8d-0660-41ba-9846-165586eca430-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/05/