le', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jacqueline Stevens', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jacqueline', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stevens', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc4544de-f5e2-5a09-9f9d-ca0876ba2f0a', 'word_count': 876, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc4544de-f5e2-5a09-9f9d-ca0876ba2f0a'}, {'abstract': 'Pentagon officials have expressed concern about being seen as picking a fight with an ally at a time when the military has plenty of adversaries to contend with.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/04/us/politics/national-guard-immigration-border.html', 'snippet': 'Pentagon officials have expressed concern about being seen as picking a fight with an ally at a time when the military has plenty of adversaries to contend with.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — A little over a year ago, when news surfaced of a Trump administration memo that proposed mobilizing as many as 100,000 National Guard troops to round up unauthorized immigrants at the southern border, a White House spokesman quickly denounced the reports as “irresponsible.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/05/us/politics/05dc-military-1/05dc-military-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/05/us/politics/05dc-military-1/05dc-military-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/05/us/politics/05dc-military-1/05dc-military-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo'