es', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Citizenship and Immigration Services (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-06T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Miriam Jordan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Miriam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jordan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/43bc867c-7045-55a3-8482-690267301b67', 'word_count': 1310, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43bc867c-7045-55a3-8482-690267301b67'}, {'abstract': 'The latest from Sophie Blackall, David Wiesner and Jillian Tamaki show the form at its sophisticated best. They’re a treat for grown-up readers, too.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/06/books/review/i-got-it-david-wiesner.html', 'snippet': 'The latest from Sophie Blackall, David Wiesner and Jillian Tamaki show the form at its sophisticated best. They’re a treat for grown-up readers, too.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Modern-day tastemakers, unlike their forebears, shower love on comics and graphic novels without a hint of condescension. This is true even of works intended mainly for children, a category that includes — let’s be honest — most of the superhero sagas that dominate pop culture’s most lucrative precincts. I’m not complaining, by the way (I love comics too), just observing. But picture books are another story. Even the genius likes of Beatrix Potter and Maurice Sendak are shunted off to the critical equivalent of the Thanksgiving kids’ table, smiled at but not often engaged with. Yet the best picture books, far from being baby food, display a pictorial sophistication that puts many graphic novels to shame; think of them as visual haiku, an art form of juxtaposition and implication, bright colors notwithstanding. And here are three examples to prove the point — books full of surface delight that also reward close reading. Kids might love them, but I’m guessing all three will resonate even more with grown-ups.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/books/review/08Handy/merlin_135858231_0a6e5aee-1b52-402e-82eb-de4299a002f0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1007, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/08/books/review/08Handy/merlin_135858231_0a6e5aee-1b52-402e-82eb-de4299a002f0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1007}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/books/review/08Handy/merlin_135858231_0a6e5aee-1b52-402e-82eb-de4299a002f0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 611, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/books/review/08Handy/merlin_135858231_0a6e5aee-1b52-402e-82eb-de4299a002f0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1221, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/books/review/08Handy/08Handy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/08/books/review/08Handy/08Handy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/books/review/08Handy/08Handy-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'If Graphic Novels Are Hip for Adults, Why Not Picture Books?', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Art of the Picture Book', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'I Got It! (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wiesner, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Hello Lighthouse (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blackall, Sophie', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'They Say Blue (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tamaki, Jillian', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-06T09:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Handy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Handy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5e94ca3-489c-56fd-8f2a-3b4a2e1bd8d5', 'word_count': 1188, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5e94ca3-489c-56fd-8f2a-3b4a2e1bd8d5'}, {'abstract': 'Never mind the logo on the storefront. Not all authorized retailers are the same.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/06/nyregion/quirks-and-gossip-in-a-corporate-cellphone-branch.html', 'snippet': 'Never mind the logo on the storefront. Not all authorized retailers are the same.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a recent Friday afternoon at an AT&T store in Park Slope, the scene was chaotic. A customer holding a bicycle helmet lingered over the iPhones, browsing; a sixtysomething man knelt on the floor, taking a selfie with a German pinscher named Polo; and a young Orthodox man wrapped a customer in tefillin, traditional Jewish prayer straps, while reading him prayers to repeat. “I get an iPhone, and I get tefillin,” said the customer, Harvey Rossel, a chiropractor and longtime local.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/nyregion/08ATT1/08ATT1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/08/nyregion/08ATT1/08ATT1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/nyregion/08ATT1/08ATT1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 686, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/nyregion/08ATT1/08ATT1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1372, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/nyregion/08ATT1/02AT&T1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/08/nyregion/08ATT1/02AT&T1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/nyregion/08ATT1/02AT&T1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Quirks and Gossip in a Corporate Cellphone Branch', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Forget the Logo. This Store Has the Quirks of Another Era.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kirtchuk, Ari', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AT&T Inc', 'rank': 2, 'maj