arly to pick up his children at their Brooklyn elementary school that he has turned the cafeteria into his ad hoc congressional campaign office, working on speeches until school lets out.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/07/nyregion/07subwayearly1/merlin_135918534_3118cb4b-96cf-4590-8a40-4d313cb61e22-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/07/nyregion/07subwayearly1/merlin_135918534_3118cb4b-96cf-4590-8a40-4d313cb61e22-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/07/nyregion/07subwayearly1/merlin_135918534_3118cb4b-96cf-4590-8a40-4d313cb61e22-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/07/nyregion/07subwayearly1/merlin_135918534_3118cb4b-96cf-4590-8a40-4d313cb61e22-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/07/nyregion/07subwayearly1/00subwayearly1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/07/nyregion/07subwayearly1/00subwayearly1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/07/nyregion/07subwayearly1/00subwayearly1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Subway Is So Late, It’s Making New Yorkers Early', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Now Early for Work, Frustrated New Yorkers Blame the Subway', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subways', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transit Systems', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Delays (Transportation)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Commuting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-06T16:01:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Maslin Nir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': 'Maslin', 'lastname': 'Nir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a02136e2-a057-548c-8777-1bc6db6f8fa9', 'word_count': 1189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a02136e2-a057-548c-8777-1bc6db6f8fa9'}, {'abstract': 'Jacqueline Winspear was mired in London gridlock when her beloved fictional detective, Maisie Dobbs, popped into her head.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/06/books/review/jacqueline-winspear-best-seller.html', 'snippet': 'Jacqueline Winspear was mired in London gridlock when her beloved fictional detective, Maisie Dobbs, popped into her head.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jacqueline Winspear was mired in London gridlock back in 2001 when her fictional detective, Maisie Dobbs, popped into her head. “Stoplights were at red as far as I could see,” the novelist remembers. “In my mind’s eye I saw a woman walk up the old wooden escalator at the Warren Street underground station, dressed in the garb of the mid-1920s. She spoke to the newspaper vendor, then made her way along the street to a building, where she took an envelope with two keys from her bag ... I was shaken out of my daydream by honking from cars stuck behind me. I could not wait to get home to start writing.” Maisie Dobbs — nurse turned private eye — now has a devoted following (Hillary Clinton is a fan), and the 14th book in the series, “To Die but Once,” debuts at No. 5 on the fiction list.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/15/books/review/15-BKS-LIST/15-BKS-LIST-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 603, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/15/books/review/15-BKS-LIST/15-BKS-LIST-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 603}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/15/books/review/15-BKS-LIST/15-BKS-LIST-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1020, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/15/books/review/15-BKS-LIST/15-BKS-LIST-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1080, 'width': 1075, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/15/books/review/15-BKS-LIST/15-BKS-LIST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/15/books/review/15-BKS-LIST/15-BKS-LIST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/15/books/review/15-BKS-LIST/15-BKS-LIST-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Traffic Jam Changed Her Life', 'kicker': 'Inside the List', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Life-Altering Traffic Jam', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-06T16:12:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tina Jordan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jordan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/3fec4ff7-20d6-570f-aafe-f107acf29cbc', 'word_count': 458, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3fec4ff7-20d6-570f-aafe-f107acf29cbc'}, {'abstract': 'Yes, it’s never been more crowded or more frustrating. But at the same time: How cool is the J train at sunset?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/06/nyregion/the-miracle-after-all-of-the-subway.html', 'snippet': 'Yes, it’s never been more crowded or more frustrating. But at the same time: How cool is the J train at sunset?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New York City subway system includes more than 6,000 cars and 665 miles of track, but when you’re riding it, every inch can seem like an epic journey. Is your express train making all local stops? Are you being held in the station because of an earlier incident? Or maybe the guy in the next seat finally achieved a 180-degree manspread. What time is it? C’mon, man. It’s always showtime. ', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/23/nyregion/00subway-promo/00subway-promo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/03/23/nyregion/00subway-promo/00subway-promo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/23/nyregion/00subway-promo/00subway-promo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/23/nyregion/00subway-promo/00subway-promo-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/23/nyregion/00subway-promo/00subway-promo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/03/23/nyregion/00subway-promo/00subway-promo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/23/nyregion/00subway-promo/00subway-promo-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Miracle, After All, of the Subway', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Miracle of the Subway', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subways', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transit Systems', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Williamsburg (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-06T16:14:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ramsay de Give and John Leland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ramsay', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'de Give', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd062f2e-afb8-5754-808a-ed4e0454ee51', 'word_count': 333, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd062f2e-afb8-5754-808a-ed4e0454ee51'}, {'abstract': 'The star tenor, along with Camilla Nylund and Andris Nelsons, discussed the opera’s long love duet, which they will perform at Carnegie Hall on April 12.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/06/arts/music/jonas-kaufmann-wagner-tristan-und-isolde-boston-symphony.html', 'snippet': 'The star tenor, along with Camilla Nylund and Andris Nelsons, discussed the opera’s long love duet, which they will perform at Carnegie Hall on April 12.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — The love duet at the heart of Wagner’s “Tristan und Isolde” makes little sense. Breathless ecstasy is conveyed through made-up, often bafflingly long words as the titular lovers declare their devotion — over and over — for 40 minutes.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/07/arts/07TRISTAN2/07TRISTAN2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/07/arts/07TRISTAN2/07TRISTAN2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/07/arts/07TRISTAN2/07TRISTAN2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/07/arts/07TRISTAN2/07TRISTAN2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/07/arts/07TRISTAN2/07TRISTAN2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/07/arts/07TRISTAN2/07TRISTAN2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/07/arts/07TRISTAN2/07TRISTAN2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jonas Kaufmann: Wagner’s ‘Tristan’ Is ‘Like a Drug’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wagner’s ‘Tristan und Isolde’ Is ‘Like a Drug’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boston Symphony Orchestra', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carnegie Hall', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nelsons, Andris', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kaufmann, Jonas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nylund, Camilla', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wagner, Richard', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Tristan und Isolde (Opera)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-06T16:21:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Barone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/73cb0355-9383-57ec-b940-f139e9ae546b', 'word_count': 975, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73cb0355-9383-57ec-b940-f139e9ae546b'}, {'abstract': 'When disaster knocks out cell towers, gas-powered drones can restore service. Verizon and AT&T are experimenting with the technology.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/06/nyregion/drone-cellphone-disaster-service.html', 'snippet': 'When disaster knocks out cell towers, gas-powered drones can restore service. Verizon and AT&T are experimenting with the technology.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Law enforcement and emergency responders have been using drones to give them an eye in the sky for years. But the unmanned aerial vehicles may soon provide ears as well. Two of the country’s largest mobile phone companies are exploring using drones as flying mobile hot spots to provide phone and other services when cell towers are down or in areas where service does not exist.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/07/nyregion/07celldrones1/merlin_135246699_54e90c9c-a48e-43af-aa95-7f6f9c16d7b7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/07/nyregion/07celldrones1/merlin_135246699_54e90c9c-a48e-43af-aa95-7f6f9c16d7b7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/07/nyregion/07celldrones1/merlin_135246699_54e90c9c-a48e-43af-aa95-7f6f9c16d7b7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/07/nyregion/07celldrones1/merlin_135246699_5