ct': 'A by-no-means exhaustive list of the things our editors (and a few contributors) find interesting on a given week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/06/t-magazine/pleasure-garden-magazine-river-cafe-cookbook-editors-picks.html', 'snippet': 'A by-no-means exhaustive list of the things our editors (and a few contributors) find interesting on a given week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The rose-themed third issue of Pleasure Garden magazine abounds with flower porn: Blush-pink petals spill out of a sculptural table arrangement, wilted bouquets decorate horses’ manes, a long-stem rose emerges from a pair of perky butt cheeks. Named after the pleasure gardens of 18th-century London — cradles of art, society and sex — the biannual British publication was conceived in 2017 to reframe the narrative around gardens and plants. “I wanted to do a magazine that didn’t tell people how to prune and grow things, but to delight in the art of the garden,” says John Tebbs, the magazine’s editor in chief and a professional gardener for 15 years. To that end, the third issue of Pleasure Garden is a 200-page rumination on the rose. One article unpacks the myth of a rose-loving empress, another captures a flower-filled parade during the Portland Rose Festival and a third visits the artist Robert Tatin’s eccentric concrete sculpture garden in Northwest France. “There is so much beauty within gardens and the natural world that I am never short of inspiration,” says Tebbs. Available online on April 18 and at Totokaelo in New York on May 19, thegardenedit.com — ZIO BARITAUX', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/06/t-magazine/06tmag-tel-slide-GN37/06tmag-tel-slide-GN37-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 471, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/06/t-magazine/06tmag-tel-slide-GN37/06tmag-tel-slide-GN37-articleLarg