': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b96aca0e-7d85-5bed-82ac-b1b07b56eab8', 'word_count': 2457, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b96aca0e-7d85-5bed-82ac-b1b07b56eab8'}, {'abstract': 'How racist federal policy brought Jim Crow to housing in the North.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/07/opinion/sunday/americas-federally-financed-ghettos.html', 'snippet': 'How racist federal policy brought Jim Crow to housing in the North.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ben Carson, the secretary of housing and urban development, showed utter contempt for his agency’s core mission last month when he proposed deleting the phrase “free from discrimination”  from the HUD mission statement. Yet Mr. Carson is not the first housing secretary to betray the landmark Fair Housing Act of 1968 — which turns 50 years old this week — by failing to enforce policies designed to prevent states and cities from using federal dollars to perpetuate segregation.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/opinion/sunday/08sun1/08sun1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/08/opinion/sunday/08sun1/08sun1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 599}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/opinion/sunday/08sun1/08sun1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1023, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/opinion/sunday/08sun1/08sun1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1502, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/opinion/sunday/08sun1/08sun1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/08/opinion/sunday/08sun1/08sun1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/opinion/sunday/08sun1/08sun1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'America’s Federally Financed Ghettos', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'America’s Federally Financed Ghettos', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Public and Subsidized Housing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Housing and Urban Development Department', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carson, Benjamin S', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-07T18:30:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/a14f7b61-e12a-57b8-bf25-47d4c24da345', 'word_count': 1414, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a14f7b61-e12a-57b8-bf25-47d4c24da345'}, {'abstract': 'They’re not just short on textbooks. They’re crumbling. They lack tornado shelters in Oklahoma and aren’t built to withstand earthquakes in Utah.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/07/opinion/sunday/americas-deathtrap-schools.html', 'snippet': 'They’re not just short on textbooks. They’re crumbling. They lack tornado shelters in Oklahoma and aren’t built to withstand earthquakes in Utah.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week, thousands of teachers in Oklahoma and Kentucky walked out of their classrooms. West Virginia teachers did the same thing in February, and Arizona may be next. They are protesting state governments that are failing to pay teachers a decent wage, replace outdated textbooks, ensure manageable class sizes and fix school buildings in need of repair.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/opinion/sunday/08peek/merlin_136383609_7276b691-96b5-4734-a79b-be44a302e2f0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/08/opinion/sunday/08peek/merlin_136383609_7276b691-96b5-4734-a79b-be44a302e2f0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/opinion/sunday/08peek/merlin_136383609_7276b691-96b5-4734-a79b-be44a302e2f0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/opinion/sunday/08peek/merlin_136383609_7276b691-96b5-4734-a79b-be44a302e2f0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/opinion/sunday/08peek/08peek-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/08/opinion/sunday/08peek/08peek-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'ca