n': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/87fc5351-e881-5863-ac26-59e7ac605345', 'word_count': 995, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87fc5351-e881-5863-ac26-59e7ac605345'}, {'abstract': 'In some salons, as many as half of the Asian-American clients are going lighter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/09/fashion/why-are-so-many-asian-american-women-bleaching-their-hair-blond.html', 'snippet': 'In some salons, as many as half of the Asian-American clients are going lighter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first time I was aware of my Asianness was when I asked my mother why I wasn’t blond. I was 5, and one of only a handful of Asian-Americans living in a predominantly white suburb in Michigan. Of course, my story is not unique — it’s an experience that’s probably shared by most American-born Asians as we shake off our perceived otherness and strive to prove our Americanness. There’s a term for it: “perpetual foreigner.”', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/12/fashion/12SKIN-leung/merlin_135716049_ace15db8-8c65-489e-95d8-ba5285299c47-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/12/fashion/12SKIN-leung/merlin_135716049_ace15db8-8c65-489e-95d8-ba5285299c47-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/12/fashion/12SKIN-leung/merlin_135716049_ace15db8-8c65-489e-95d8-ba5285299c47-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/12/fashion/12SKIN-leung/merlin_135716049_ace15db8-8c65-489e-95d8-ba5285299c47-su