': 'subject', 'value': 'Data-Mining and Database Marketing', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Russian Interference in 2016 US Elections and Ties to Trump Associates', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-10T19:58:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheera Frenkel and Linda Qiu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheera', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Frenkel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Linda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Qiu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f062374-8939-5e1a-9bf7-31c08ba991ac', 'word_count': 1208, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f062374-8939-5e1a-9bf7-31c08ba991ac'}, {'abstract': 'A selection of books published this week; plus, a peek at what our colleagues around the newsroom are reading.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/10/books/review/new-noteworthy-sasha-weiss.html', 'snippet': 'A selection of books published this week; plus, a peek at what our colleagues around the newsroom are reading.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MAN VS. BABY By Matt Coyne. (Scribner, paper, $17.) Coyne’s exasperated Facebook post about early parenthood (a baby’s cry is like “having the inside of your skull sandpapered by an angry Viking”) has been viewed 15 million times. That initial rant is now a funny memoir and parenting manual for the equally “inept.” THE HOPE CIRCUIT By Martin E. P. Seligman. (PublicAffairs, $28.) A major figure in the world of psychology, Seligman describes how he turned his practice from one that focused on eliminating misery to one centered on gratitude, resilience and hope. THE PLANT MESSIAH By Carlos Magdalena. (Doubleday, $26.95.) In a world whose ecology is changing so fast, only a special kind of obsessive would concern himself with saving endangered plant species. That’s Magdalena, the senior botanical horticulturist at the Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew, who recounts adventures that have taken him from the Amazon to the jungles of Mauritius on a quest to preserve as much flora as he can. EVERYTHING IS BROKEN UP AND DANCES By Guido Maria Brera and Edoardo Nesi. Translated by Antony Shugaar. (Other, $18.95.) Nesi and Brera rail against globalization and its deleterious effects on a country like Italy. Nesi, who had to close his grandfather’s decades-old textile business, understands the problems firsthand. WHAT THE FUTURE LOOKS LIKE Edited By Jim Al-Khalili. (The Experiment, paper, $14.95.) In this collection, eminent writers flesh out the future of science and technology, from genomics to robotics to synthetic biology.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/15/books/review/15NewNoteworthy/15NewNoteworthy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 298, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/15/books/review/15NewNoteworthy/15NewNoteworthy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 298}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/15/books/review/15NewNoteworthy/15NewNoteworthy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 509, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/15/books/review/15NewNoteworthy/15NewNoteworthy-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1018, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': 