 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/12/books/review/8-new-books-we-recommend-this-week.html', 'snippet': 'Suggested reading from critics and editors at The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Because we work in newspapers, and are as susceptible to buzzy jargon as the next person, we sometimes refer to “the mix” when we try to explain how we make our assignments or schedule our reviews each week. It’s an amorphous term, redolent of Chex cereal or disco floors or cocktail bars, and not easy to define in the context of books journalism. But this week’s recommended titles offer a perfect illustration of the concept: On the surface, these eight books have little in common, but taken together they add up to a broad snapshot of life today. One novel looks at gun culture; another explores how feminism has changed over the years. There’s the memoir of a woman raised among survivalists, and a look at the Syrian civil war, and a deep investigative dive into the 2016 presidential election and possible Russian interference. Less topical, but still fascinating, is Yunte Huang’s thorough history of the 19th-century conjoined twins Chang and Eng. Finally, because sometimes all you want is an original mind or a great escapist plot, we’ve also found a surreal story collection and a retro thriller and introduced them into the — well, you know.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/15/books/review/15edchoice-covers/15edchoice-covers-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 294, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/15/books/review/15edchoice-covers/15edchoice-covers-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 294}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/15/books/review/15edchoice-covers/15edchoice-cove