kota’s primary was not until June 4, and no one would pay much attention because that was the day of the all-important California primary.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/16/opinion/16widmerWeb/16widmerWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 403, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/16/opinion/16widmerWeb/16widmerWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 403}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/16/opinion/16widmerWeb/16widmerWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 688, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/16/opinion/16widmerWeb/16widmerWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1376, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/16/opinion/16widmerWeb/16widmerWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/16/opinion/16widmerWeb/16widmerWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/16/opinion/16widmerWeb/16widmerWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Robert Kennedy Went to Pine Ridge', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 1968', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Native Americans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rural Areas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kennedy, Robert Francis', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pine Ridge Indian Reservation (SD)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-16T09:45:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ted Widmer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ted', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Widmer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/e87cf1b9-b56a-5ef9-9e96-c7b89068004e', 'word_count': 1613, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e87cf1b9-b56a-5ef9-9e96-c7b89068004e'}, {'abstract': 'One cure for a crisis of faith: a Sunday school lesson taught by the former president.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/16/opinion/church-jimmy-carter.html', 'snippet': 'One cure for a crisis of faith: a Sunday school lesson taught by the former president.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I knew I was in trouble as soon as I pulled into the parking lot of the Quality Inn in Americus, Ga., last Saturday night. It was long past dark but I could see well enough to note the out-of-state license plates — New Mexico, Pennsylvania, California, Alaska — places far from this peanut-farming land.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/16/opinion/16renklSub/16renklSub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 387, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/16/opinion/16renklSub/16renklSub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 387}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/16/opinion/16renklSub/16renklSub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 661, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/16/opinion/16renklSub/16renklSub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1322, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/16/opinion/16renklSub/16renklSub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/16/opinion/16renklSub/16renklSub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/16/opinion/16renklSub/16renklSub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Going to Church With Jimmy Carter', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Going to Church With Jimmy Carter', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christians and Christianity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carter, Jimmy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-16T09:46:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margaret Renkl', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margaret', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Renkl', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/212db70a-0144-53a4-a2cd-76a31e823f19', 'word_count': 1189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/212db70a-0144-53a4-a2cd-76a31e823f19'}, {'abstract': 'From Broadway to downtown experimental shows, the city’s theater ruled supreme. Here are some of its alumni, including Sarah Jessica Parker, Willem Dafoe, Cynthia Nixon and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/16/t-magazine/broadway-1980s-actors-sarah-jessica-parker-willem-dafoe.html', 'snippet': 'From Broadway to downtown experimental shows, the city’s theater ruled supreme. Here are some of its alumni, including Sarah Jessica Parker, Willem Dafoe, Cynthia Nixon and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After I moved to New York in the early ’80s, my first Broadway play was the head-banging “Hurlyburly,” by David Rabe, which did to theater what Julian Schnabel was at the same moment doing to plates. In the show, William Hurt, Ron Silver and Harvey Keitel all play showroom model prototypes of a sexual psychosis we now call “toxic masculinity,” as provoked by Cynthia Nixon, Sigourney Weaver and Judith Ivey. You can see them all now as icons, but back then they were like ions — in kinetic motion, orchestrated by the shaper of all molecules, director Mike Nichols. Consider also that a gamine Nixon, 18 at the time, was in two shows concurrently: She had to race back and forth between this raging behemoth of an American play and a cleareyed British one, Tom Stoppard’s “The Real Thing,” just a few blocks away, where she provided wisdom beyond her years to Jeremy Irons and Glenn Close, again agitated into motion by Nichols.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '109', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/05/t-magazine/05tmag-group-slide-QT9P/05tmag-group-slide-QT9P-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/05/t-magazine/05tmag-group-slide-QT9P/05tmag-group-slide-QT9P-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/05/t-magazine/05tmag-g