bnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/18/science/18TESS/18TESS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Watch as NASA and SpaceX Launch TESS, a Planet-Hunting Satellite', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Space and Astronomy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transiting Exoplanet Survey Satellite (Spacecraft)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Extraterrestrial Life', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Aeronautics and Space Administration', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-16T16:53:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dennis Overbye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dennis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Overbye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8b80d7ec-e8fb-5c5f-9992-003866796f23', 'word_count': 478, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8b80d7ec-e8fb-5c5f-9992-003866796f23'}, {'abstract': 'Styles from Manhattan’s age of excess — from explosive more-is-more arrangements to humble baby’s breath and tacky tropicals — are relevant again, nearly four decades later.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/16/t-magazine/flowers-1980s.html', 'snippet': 'Styles from Manhattan’s age of excess — from explosive more-is-more arrangements to humble baby’s breath and tacky tropicals — are relevant again, nearly four decades later.', 'lead_paragraph': '2018 is shaping up to be the year of the anthurium, formerly a lead contender for world’s most despised flower. Should you need reminding, the anthurium is that plastic-y looking plant with a fingerlike spadix rising out of a flat shiny red bract, which has been referred to, for reasons that require no explanation, as “penis on a platter.” This controversial flower is just one of a host of formerly derided flora being reclaimed from the slush pile of floristry.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/t-magazine/03tmag-hicart-slide-73Y7/03tmag-hicart-slide-73Y7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 435, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/t-magazine/03tmag-hicart-slide-73Y7/03tmag-hicart-slide-73Y7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 435}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/t-magazine/03tmag-hicart-slide-73Y7/03tmag-hicart-slide-73Y7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 742, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/t-magazine/03tmag-hicart-slide-73Y7/03tmag-hicart-slide-73Y7-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1484, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/t-magazine/03tmag-hicart-slide-73Y7/03tmag-hicart-slide-73Y7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/03/t-magazine/03tmag-hicart-slide-73Y7/03tmag-hicart-slide-73Y7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/t-magazine/03tmag-hicart-slide-73Y7/03tmag-hicart-slide-73Y7-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Return of Decadent ’80s Flowers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Flowers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flowers and Plants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nineteen Hundred Eighties', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-16T16:56:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deborah Needleman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deborah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Needleman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/53fd3b6c-a31d-5773-859b-1c3c94a28617', 'word_count': 679, 'uri': 'nyt://article/53fd3b6c-a31d-5773-859b-1c3c94a28617'}, {'abstract': 'When hip-hop culture started to spread in the 1980s, it changed the city in more ways than one.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/16/t-magazine/hip-hop-music-1980s.html', 'snippet': 'When hip-hop culture started to spread in the 1980s, it changed the city in more ways than one.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ON VALENTINE’S DAY 1981 Deborah Harry was the headlining musical guest on “Saturday Night Live.” A product of the fertile downtown Manhattan musical scene, centered around the CBGB club on the Bowery, she had gone pop with a series of hit singles, including Blondie’s “Rapture,” which reached No. 1 in 1981. The track, a tribute to an uptown musical culture that most Americans viewed as a curiosity or fad (if they had any awareness of it at all), name-checked a few seminal New York figures, including Grandmaster Flash, the pioneering D.J. whose group the Furious Five formed in 1976 and broke musical ground at house parties in the South Bronx before cracking into the Top 100 in 1982 with the socially conscious rap anthem “The Message,” and Fab 5 Freddy, a Brooklyn-based graffiti artist who acted as a liaison between street artists, the commercial art world and downtown clubs. At the urging of Blondie’s Harry and the band’s guitarist Chris Stein, “S.N.L.” booked the Bronx’s Funky 4 + 1 (four male rappers and pioneering female M.C. Sha-Rock) to perform their single “That’s the Joint” for the show’s final number of the night. Harry’s rapped verse on “Rapture” was, for most people, a proxy introduction to hip-hop, but it was the Funky 4 + 1 that gave network television viewers the first true taste of what young New Yorkers had been experiencing in parks, subways and schoolyards for several years already.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '46', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/t-magazine/03tmag-hicart-slide-9I19/03tmag-hicart-slide-9I19-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 436, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/t-magazine/03tmag-hicart-slide-9I19/03tmag-hicart-slide-9I19-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 436}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/t-magazine/03tmag-hicart-slide-9I19/03tmag-hicart-slide-9I19-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 744, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/t-magazine/03tmag-hicart-slide-9I19/03tmag-hicart-slide-9I19-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1489, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/t-magazine/03tmag-hicart-slide-9I19/03tmag-hicart-slide-9I19-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/03/t-magazine/03tmag-hicart-slide-9I19/03tmag-hicart-slide-9I19-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/t-magazine/03tmag-hicart-slide-9I19/03tmag-hicart-slide-9I19-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Hip-Hop Transformed New York', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Music, Language, Downtown — and White People', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rap and Hip-Hop', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grandmaster Flash (1958- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harry, Deborah', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-16T16:57:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nelson George', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nelson', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'George', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/89fdb4b3-1b8a-5abd-b64d-e76edae60670', 'word_count': 844, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89fdb4b3-1b8a-5abd-b64d-e76edae60670'}, {'abstract': 'The designer’s debut, in 1981, marked the end of an era and the beginning of a new wave of New York design.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/16/t-magazine/fashion-1980s-carolina-herrera.html', 'snippet': 'The designer’s debut, in 1981, marked the end of an era and the beginning of a new wave of New York design.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ON APRIL 27, 1981, Carolina Herrera — a remarkably well-dressed, well-liked society figure, Studio 54 fixture, mother of four and Warhol muse — presented her first collection as a fashion designer. It was a career move suggested by Diana Vreeland, the former editor of American Vogue who was by then a curator to the Metropolitan Museum of Art’s Costume Institute in Manhattan. “I wanted to design textiles,” the 79-year-old Herrera recalls. “I came to see Diana, and I told her my story and asked whether she liked the idea or not. And she said, ‘My God, that’s very boring. Why don’t you do something else, connected with fashion? Why don’t you do a collection?’\u2009” So she did. It was around 20 outfits, on a group of models corralled with help from her friend, the designer Bill Blass. It was shown at the Metropolitan Club, an august private social club founded in 1891 by J.P. Morgan and housed on New York’s East 60th Street — the first time the venue had permitted a fashion show within its walls. Musicians played Cole Porter. It was an event.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '54', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/11/t-magazine/oakImage-1523480497766/oakImage-1523480497766-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/11/t-magazine/oakImage-1523480497766/oakImage-1523480497766-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 800}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/11/t-magazine/oakImage-1523480497766/oakImage-1523480497766-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/11/t-magazine/oakImage-1523480497766/oakImage-1523480497766-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1600, 'width': 1200, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/11/t-magazine/oakImage-1523480497766/oakImage-1523480497766-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/11/t-magazine/oakImage-1523480497766/oakImage-1523480497766-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/11/t-magazine/oakImage-1523480497766/oakImage-1523480497766-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Carolina Herrera’s Very First Show — and What It Meant for Fashion ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'American Fashion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nineteen Hundred Eighties', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Herrera, Carolina', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-16T16:59:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexander Fury', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexander', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fury', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4888520a-7c07-50b8-9cf0-647dfecf1b95', 'word_count': 921, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4888520a-7c07-50b8-9cf0-647dfecf1b95'}, {'abstract': 'The fashion designer stepped away from the role of creative director at her eponymous label this year. She appears on one of the Culture issue covers — and in this video inspired by her final bow.', 'w