jor': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Buildings (Structures)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Building (Construction)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Area Planning and Renewal', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Zoning', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-17T08:16:11+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Fuller, Anjali Singhvi and Josh Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fuller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Anjali', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Singhvi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Josh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/abbd181e-0a5e-5d7a-9f79-17302318b22d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/abbd181e-0a5e-5d7a-9f79-17302318b22d'}, {'abstract': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on withholding information from a family member that may be painful to hear and handling unfairness at work.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/17/magazine/was-it-ok-to-let-an-aging-relative-believe-my-mother-was-still-alive.html', 'snippet': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on withholding information from a family member that may be painful to hear and handling unfairness at work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When my beloved 92-year-old mother became gravely ill and passed away last May, a first cousin who lives close by requested that I not tell her 92-year-old mother (my mother’s ex-sister-in-law) about the illness (and death) should I see her in the park. She felt that it would be too difficult for her mother to accept and might cause mental and physical problems. During the summer, when I did cross paths with my aunt, she asked me how my mother was. Hesitating, I said that everything was just fine. Afterward, I felt bad and angry that I had lied to my aunt; I felt she should have been told. My aunt wasn’t even able to say goodbye to my mother during her illness or to attend the funeral and memorial services. I promised myself that should I see my aunt again, I would inform her in a very compassionate way about my mom’s passing. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/22/magazine/22mag-ethicist/22mag-22ethicist-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 792, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/22/magazine/22mag-ethicist/22mag-22ethicist-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 792}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/22/magazine/22mag-ethicist/22mag-22ethicist-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 776, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/22/magazine/22mag-ethicist/22mag-22ethicist-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1400, 'width': 1060, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/22/magazine/22mag-ethicist/22mag-22ethicist-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/22/magazine/22mag-ethicist/22mag-22ethicist-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thu