k': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Haring, Keith', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kramer, Larry (1935- )', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-17T12:33:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frank Bruni', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bruni', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/22a52b82-baea-58e2-9d5b-f4f7ca74a927', 'word_count': 1701, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22a52b82-baea-58e2-9d5b-f4f7ca74a927'}, {'abstract': 'For a short time in the early ’80s, the Manhattan neighborhood was the epicenter of experimental art. Jeff Koons, Peter Halley, Ashley Bickerton, Joan Wallace and Barbara Bloom remember the moment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/17/t-magazine/east-village-artist-jeff-koons-peter-halley.html', 'snippet': 'For a short time in the early ’80s, the Manhattan neighborhood was the epicenter of experimental art. Jeff Koons, Peter Halley, Ashley Bickerton, Joan Wallace and Barbara Bloom remember the moment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By 1981 in New York City, the contemporary art scene was a booming business, with SoHo as its epicenter. Dealers like Leo Castelli, Ileana Sonnabend and Mary Boone championed artists such as David Salle and Julian Schnabel, whom critics labeled Neo-Expressionists, and whose style appealed to a growing art market willing to pay large sums of money for young painters. But just a few blocks and a world away — in the East Village — a smaller but no less important community of galleries was emerging as well, a kind of conceptual and anti-commercial satellite orbiting the art world’s mainstream.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '102', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/04/t-magazine/04tmag-eastvillage-slide-UBL0/04tmag-eastvillage-slide-UBL0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 564, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/04/t-magazine/04tmag-eastvillage-slide-UBL0/04tmag-eastvillage-slide-UBL0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 564}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/04/t-magazine/04tmag-eastvillage-slide-UBL0/04tmag-eastvillage-slide-UBL0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 962, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/04/t-magazine/04tmag-eastvillage-slide-UBL0/04tmag-eastvillage-slide-UBL0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1924, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/04/t-magazine/04tmag-eastvillage-slide-UBL0/04tmag-eastvillage-slide-UBL0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/04/t-magazine/04tmag-eastvillage-slide-UBL0/04tmag-eastvillage-slide-UBL0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/04/t-magazine/04tmag-eastvillage-slide-UBL0/04tmag-eastvillage-slide-UBL0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Artists Who Defined the East Village’s Avant-Garde Scene', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'There Went the Neighborhood', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nineteen Hundred Eighties', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koons, Jeff', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wallace, Joan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'East Village (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-17T12:37:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By M.H. Miller', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13674094-6471-5df2-b230-d4cf323c8258', 'word_count': 3949, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13674094-6471-5df2-b230-d4cf323c8258'}, {'abstract': 'New Yorkers declared their allegiance to a neighborhood via their decorating choices — and proved that an apartment should be the ultimate expression of its inhabitant’s beliefs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/17/t-magazine/interior-design-1980s.html', 'snippet': 'New Yorkers declared their allegiance to a neighborhood via their decorating choices — and proved that an apartment should be the ultimate expression of its inhabitant’s beliefs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Billy Joel’s “Uptown Girl,” released in 1983, was, on the surface, about the working-class Long Island rocker wooing the rich model Christie Brinkley. But the song was more deeply about a famed Manhattan boundary, one insurmountable at the time: “Uptown” was shorthand for the well-lit East Side corridor where nouvelle society, powered by the Wall Street boom, resided in splendor. “Downtown” was the shadowy province beneath 14th Street — a countercultural warren of tenement walk-ups and industrial loft spaces.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '130', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/05/t-magazine/05tmag-design1/05tmag-design1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 443, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/05/t-magazine/05tmag-design1/05tmag-design1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 443}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/05/t-magazine/05tmag-design1/05tmag-design1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 756, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/05/t-magazine/05tmag-design1/05tmag-design1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1511, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/05/t-magazine/05tmag-design1/05tmag-design1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/05/t-magazine/05tmag-design1/05tmag-design1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/05/t-magazine/05tmag-design1/05tmag-design1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Uptown or Downtown? In the Early ’80s, It Made All the Difference ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'This Must Be The Place', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chairs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tables', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Furniture', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nineteen Hundred Eighties', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-17T12:40:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nancy Hass', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hass', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cefe78f7-edc6-5a07-ab7f-2014126bbac6', 'word_count': 862, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cefe78f7-edc6-5a07-ab7f-2014126bbac6'}, {'abstract': 'Notable locals, including Dapper Dan, Tama Janowitz and Brad Gooch, on their homes, hangouts and routes around the city.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/17/t-magazine/80s-nyc-map.html', 'snippet': 'Notable locals, including Dapper Dan, Tama Janowitz and Brad Gooch, on their homes, hangouts and routes around the city.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The era might have been old New York’s last real gasp — a time when the very streets, dirty and unsafe as they were, seemed infused with possibility. Here, notable locals revisit their routes and routines, from lunch on the Upper East to nighttime sojourns to then-emerging neighborhoods like TriBeCa.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '132', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/17/t-magazine/17tmag-map/17tmag-map-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 342, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/17/t-magazine/17tmag-map/17tmag-map-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 342}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/17/t-magazine/17tmag-map/17tmag-map-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 584, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/17/t-m