rial': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/899581a5-54fd-50ca-b37c-bb7bcb2e080b', 'word_count': 1065, 'uri': 'nyt://article/899581a5-54fd-50ca-b37c-bb7bcb2e080b'}, {'abstract': 'Mrs. Bush, who died on Tuesday, presented herself as both reasonable and reasonably conservative.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/17/opinion/barbara-bush-a-first-lady-without-apologies.html', 'snippet': 'Mrs. Bush, who died on Tuesday, presented herself as both reasonable and reasonably conservative.', 'lead_paragraph': 'She knew who she was, and she saw no need to apologize for it. In the spring of 1990, the administration of Wellesley College — the alma mater, as it happened, of Hillary Rodham Clinton — invited Barbara Bush, then the first lady of the United States, to speak at commencement and receive an honorary degree. Students at the women’s college protested, declaring in a petition that Mrs. Bush had “gained recognition through the achievements of her husband,” and adding that Wellesley “teaches us that we will be rewarded on the basis of our own merit, not on that of a spouse.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/18/opinion/18meacham/18meacham-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/18/opinion/18meacham/18meacham-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/18/opinion/18meacham/18meacham-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/18/opinion/18meacham/18meacham-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 