k off. But when you earned her affection, it meant all the more. You knew it was real.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/18/opinion/barbara-bush-no-nonsense.html', 'snippet': 'Barbara Bush gave it to you with the bark off. But when you earned her affection, it meant all the more. You knew it was real.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I met her in 1981, when I went to work for her husband as a speechwriter. I was a bit in awe of her — who wasn’t? — and, I confess, a bit scared. A few weeks in, I wrote a speech that went over well, in no small part due to George H. W. Bush’s solid delivery. After the event as we boarded Air Force Two, Barbara Bush spotted me and said, “That’s the best speech he’s ever given.” I replied, “Well, we all worked hard on that speech.” Her face went stony as she brushed past my seat. She said, “Oh, don’t be such a Pollyanna.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/18/opinion/00buckley/00buckley-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 406, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/18/opinion/00buckley/00buckley-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 406}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/18/opinion/00buckley/00buckley-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 693, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/18/opinion/00buckley/00buckley-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1387, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/18/opinion/00buckley/00buckley-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/18/opinion/00buckley/00buckley-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/18/opinion/00buckley/00buckley-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mrs. No-Nonsense', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mrs. No-Nonsense', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, Barbara', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-18T09:45:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Buckley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d4d259b-512f-558b-b49b-e7c5764432a7', 'word_count': 771, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d4d259b-512f-558b-b49b-e7c5764432a7'}, {'abstract': 'She had a big, antique, rich-person’s house in Connecticut, where you could find fun, cool, opulent relief in summer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/18/opinion/the-last-day-i-spent-with-gilda-radner.html', 'snippet': 'She had a big, antique, rich-person’s house in Connecticut, where you could find fun, cool, opulent relief in summer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1975, at the brand-new “Saturday Night Live,” Lorne Michaels, with a tiny budget, hired three superb women to join the cast — Jane Curtin, Laraine Newman and Gilda Radner. Sketch comedy requires performers who can shed their personalities instantly. With characteristic prescience, Lorne saw this ability in those three. (Today, female comedians are often stand-ups — Ellen DeGeneres, Samantha Bee — who don’t morph into someone else in the middle of their shows.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/18/opinion/18millerWeb/merlin_136985433_dc6574e8-d9e0-4f1f-8f0b-db7dee8dc989-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/18/opinion/18millerWeb/merlin_136985433_dc6574e8-d9e0-4f1f-8f0b-db7dee8dc989-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/18/opinion/18millerWeb/merlin_136985433_dc6574e8-d9e0-4f1f-8f0b-db7dee8dc989-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/18/opinion/18millerWeb/merlin_136985433_dc6574e8-d9e0-4f1f-8f0b-db7dee8dc989-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/18/opinion/18millerWeb/18millerWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/18/opinion/18millerWeb/18millerWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/18/opinion/18millerWeb/18millerWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline'