, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARMS CONTROL AND LIMITATION AND DISARMAMENT', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage and Intelligence Services', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-18T14:29:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Washington', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Landler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Landler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/35fba317-63ae-52fb-b19c-54407133bba1', 'word_count': 1467, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35fba317-63ae-52fb-b19c-54407133bba1'}, {'abstract': 'Why is President Trump so determined to prevent spouses of H-1B visa holders from living the American dream?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/18/opinion/immigration-india-tech-visa.html', 'snippet': 'Why is President Trump so determined to prevent spouses of H-1B visa holders from living the American dream?', 'lead_paragraph': 'I came to America from India at age 23. That was in 1985, a golden age of immigration to this country. It didn’t feel like it, though, because it still took my husband, a medical physicist, and me a good seven years to trade up from our student visas for an H-1B for him and an H-4 spouse visa for me. Eventually we got the ultimate prize: green cards — which, to our amusement, weren’t even green back then.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/18/opinion/18dalmia/18dalmia-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 417, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/18/opinion/18dalmia/18dalmia-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 417}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type'