(Residential)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-19T09:45:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas B. Edsall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/7264969c-1470-51f1-a879-6fb9eae8b3a1', 'word_count': 2380, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7264969c-1470-51f1-a879-6fb9eae8b3a1'}, {'abstract': 'Our technology sector is good at innovation precisely because it is not dominated by the state.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/19/opinion/silicon-valley-military-contract.html', 'snippet': 'Our technology sector is good at innovation precisely because it is not dominated by the state.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is Silicon Valley going to war? In 2013, Amazon beat IBM for a contract to host the United States intelligence community’s data cloud. Microsoft now markets Azure Government Secret, its cloud-computing service designed specifically for federal and local governments, to the Defense Department and intelligence agencies. And last year, Google signed a contract with the Pentagon for Project Maven, a pilot program to accelerate the military’s use of artificial intelligence.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/19/opinion/19malcomson/19malcomson-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/19/opinion/19malcomson/19malcomson-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/19/opinion/19malcomson/19malcomson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_n