None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/20/arts/20senses-print1/merlin_136974153_e1e1431a-ea7e-4690-9ed8-d92205244ea7-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/20/arts/20senses-print1/merlin_136974153_e1e1431a-ea7e-4690-9ed8-d92205244ea7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/20/arts/20senses-print1/merlin_136974153_e1e1431a-ea7e-4690-9ed8-d92205244ea7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/20/arts/20senses-print1/merlin_136974153_e1e1431a-ea7e-4690-9ed8-d92205244ea7-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At This Museum Show, You’re Encouraged to Follow Your Nose', 'kicker': 'Design Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'You Don’t Really Need  To See This', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Touch (Sense)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Senses and Sensation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smell (Olfaction)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cooper Hewitt, Smithsonian Design Museum', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-19T17:30:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Kimmelman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kimmelman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/63ec8252-60af-5a68-8733-d0ecd6e053ba', 'word_count': 1050, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63ec8252-60af-5a68-8733-d0ecd6e053ba'}, {'abstract': 'Suggested reading from critics and editors at The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/19/books/review/11-new-books-we-recommend-this-week.html', 'snippet': 'Suggested reading from critics and editors at The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With this week’s selection of titles, we put ourselves into the hands of experts: expert storytellers, expert thinkers, expert biographers of expert subjects. For instance? As the founding curator of the New York Public Library’s photography collection, Julia Van Haaften is a leading authority on the city’s rich photographic heritage — surely the right person to have produced the first major biography of Berenice Abbott, the larger-than-life artist who captured and defined New York City in some of the 20th century’s most arresting images. Read that, and spend some time lingering over the photos. Also for instance: We recommend fiction by the celebrated masters Thomas McGuane and Richard Powers — whose new novel, “The Overstory,” is reviewed by Barbara Kingsolver, speaking of experts — and nonfiction by Hannah Arendt (about politics), Barbara Ehrenreich (about mortality) and Ross Douthat (about the Catholic Church under Pope Francis). Other choices include a family memoir, a debut novel about a single gay mother, a ghost story and a Nordic thriller with shades of “The Silence of the Lambs.” And if the Abbott biography gets you thinking about the character and destiny of cities, you might want to settle in with Ben Austen’s “High-Risers,” about the ill-fated Cabrini-Green projects in Chicago. It’s not a happy story, but it’s an important one, and Austen, a journalist who frequently writes about urban planning and housing issues, tells it expertly and well.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/22/books/review/22ed-covers/22ed-covers-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 272, 'width': 575, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/22/books/review/22ed-covers/22ed-covers-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 272, 'width': 575, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/22/books/review/22ed-covers/22ed-covers-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/22/books/review/22ed-covers/22ed-covers-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/22/books/review/22ed-covers/22ed-covers-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '11 New Books We Recommend This Week', 'kicker': 'Editors’ Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Staff Picks From the Book Review', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-19T17:32:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/9995a3d8-0d8c-5bff-9240-7867fad38d7d', 'word_count': 1411, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9995a3d8-0d8c-5bff-9240-7867fad38d7d'}, {'abstract': 'At Vassar and then at Yale’s Medical School, Dr. Hamburg blazed trails for black women. Then came a distinguished career in child development.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/19/obituaries/beatrix-hamburg-barrier-breaking-scholar-is-dead-at-94.html', 'snippet': 'At Vassar and then at Yale’s Medical School, Dr. Hamburg blazed trails for black women. Then came a distinguished career in child development.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Beatrix Hamburg, who after breaking racial barriers at two major colleges became an important researcher in child development and psychology, working on subjects like school violence and peer counseling for students, died on April 15 in Washington. She was 94.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/24/obituaries/24hamburg-print/20hamburg1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 803, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/24/obituaries/24hamburg-print/20hamburg1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 803}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/24/obituaries/24hamburg-print/20hamburg1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 765, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/24/obituaries/24hamburg-print/20hamburg1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1403, 'width': 1048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/24/obituaries/24hamburg-print/20hamburg1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {