': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-29ontech-t_CA0-alpha/29mag-29ontech-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1762, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-29ontech-t_CA0-alpha/29mag-29ontech-t_CA0-alpha-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-29ontech-t_CA0-alpha/29mag-29ontech-t_CA0-alpha-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-29ontech-t_CA0-alpha/29mag-29ontech-t_CA0-alpha-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Facebook and the ‘Dead Body’ Problem', 'kicker': 'On Technology', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cambridge Analytica', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zuckerberg, Mark E', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-24T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gideon Lewis-Kraus', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gideon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lewis-Kraus', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/57197628-244f-52ee-9f9c-51696494183e', 'word_count': 1422, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57197628-244f-52ee-9f9c-51696494183e'}, {'abstract': 'A selection of books published this week; plus, a peek at what our colleagues around the newsroom are reading.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/24/books/review/new-noteworthy-julie-bosman.html', 'snippet': 'A selection of books published this week; plus, a peek at what our colleagues around the newsroom are reading.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FACTFULNESS By Hans Rosling with Ola Rosling and Anna Rosling Ronnlund. (Flatiron, $27.99.) Rosling, who died last year, was an expert on international health who lamented the information bubbles that he feared surrounded too many of us. Burst them, he argues in this book, and our general pessimism about the state of the world will also ease — things are much better than they seem. NEAPOLITAN CHRONICLES By Anna Maria Ortese. (New Vessel, paper, $16.95.) This collection of writing and reportage about Naples was a major inspiration for Elena Ferrante. Ortese’s portrait of the Italian city just after World War II is of a place of poverty and desperation. LEAR By Harold Bloom. (Scribner, $24.) Bloom’s understanding of Lear as a character has shifted with age, allowing him to appreciate aspects of the old king he couldn’t see at 17 or 40. This focused study showcases the erudition of one of our most eminent Shakespeare scholars. THE KREMLIN BALL By Curzio Malaparte. (New York Review, paper, $15.95.) This is a glimpse of 1920s Moscow, among the Soviet high society. It’s the aftertaste of the revolution. Published posthumously, Malaparte’s court chronicle captures Stalin as the surveyor of every intrigue and scandal from his nightly opera box. THE VIEW FROM FLYOVER COUNTRY By Sarah Kendzior. (Flatiron, paper, $12.99.) A St. Louis-based journalist, Kendzior has been called “a Cassandra in Trumpland,” analyzing in these short essays the social trends and discontent in Middle America that built the president’s base.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/books/review/29NewNoteworthy0/29NewNoteworthy0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 278, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/29/books/review/29NewNoteworthy0/29NewNoteworthy0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 278}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/books/review/29NewNoteworthy0/29NewNoteworthy0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 474, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/books/review/29NewNoteworthy0/29NewNoteworthy0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 948, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/books/review/29NewNoteworthy0/29NewNoteworthy0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/29/books/review/29NewNoteworthy0/29NewNoteworthy0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/books/review/29NewNoteworthy0/29NewNoteworthy0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New & Noteworthy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New &amp; Noteworthy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-24T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e12db22-b553-5571-a2a2-1faae165b2cb', 'word_count': 446, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e12db22-b553-5571-a2a2-1faae165b2cb'}, {'abstract': 'In inquiries on Benghazi and Russia and beyond, the California congressman has displayed a deep mistrust of the expert consensus on reality — a disposition that has helped him make friends in the current White House.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/24/magazine/how-devin-nunes-turned-the-house-intelligence-committee-inside-out.html', 'snippet': 'In inquiries on Benghazi and Russia and beyond, the California congressman has displayed a deep mistrust of the expert consensus on reality — a disposition that has helped him make friends in the current White House.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Late August 2016, Donald Trump paid a visit to Tulare, Calif., a small city in the agricultural Central Valley and an unlikely stop for a Republican presidential campaign. California is a solidly blue state, and although Trump was in Tulare to speak at a fund-raiser, the $2,700 that most guests ponied up to attend hardly seemed substantial enough to justify the presence of a busy candidate. (At a fund-raiser Trump attended in Silicon Valley the day before, guests paid $25,000 a head.) At least one senior Trump campaign official argued against the trip, deeming it a colossal waste of time.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '42', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-29nunes-t_CA0-copy/29mag-29nunes-t_CA0-copy-articleLarge-v7.jpg', 'height': 613, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-29nunes-t_CA0-copy/29mag-29nunes-t_CA0-copy-articleLarge-v7.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 613}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-29nunes-t_CA0-copy/29mag-29nunes-t_CA0-copy-jumbo-v7.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1003, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-29nunes-t_CA0-copy/29mag-29nunes-t_CA0-copy-superJumbo-v7.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2006, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-29nunes-t_CA0-copy/29mag-29nunes-t_CA0-copy-thumbStandard-v5.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-29nunes-t_CA0-copy/29mag-29nunes-t_CA0-copy-thumbStandard-v5.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-29nunes-t_CA0-copy/29mag-29nunes-t_CA0-copy-thumbLarge-v5.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Devin Nunes Turned the House Intelligence Committee Inside Out', 'kicker': 'Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Truth Is Out There', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Russian Interference in 2016 US Elections and Ties to Trump Associates', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House Committee on Intelligence', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nunes, Devin G', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Azores Islands', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-24T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jason Zengerle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zengerle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba8a7715-2942-5540-aaf3-bb63687b461c', 'word_count': 6780, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba8a7715-2942-5540-aaf3-bb63687b461c'}, {'abstract': 'The 39th president of the United States on international relations and life after cancer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/24/magazine/jimmy-carter-knows-what-north-korea-wants.html', 'snippet': 'The 39th president of the United States on international relations and life after cancer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In your new book, “Faith: A Journey for All,” you write that despite believing you had only weeks to live following a cancer diagnosis in 2015, you had no fear of dying. Do you believe in an afterlife? Yes. I’m perfectly willing to leave it up to God. I didn’t have anything to do with when I was born or who my parents were, and I trust God also with the question about afterlife.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '54', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-talk1/29mag-talk1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-talk1/29mag-talk1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-talk1/29mag-talk1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-talk1/29mag-talk1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-talk1/29mag-talk1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-talk1/29mag-talk1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-talk1/29mag-talk1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jimmy Carter Knows What North Korea Wants', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jimmy Carter Knows What North Korea Wants', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carter, Jimmy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Faith: A Journey for All (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-24T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Amira', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amira', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8a7d392-0f4b-596b-9e4a-cc045fd81470', 'word_count': 684, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8a7d392-0f4b-596b-9e4a-cc045fd81470'}, {'abstract': 'Mayonnaise from your fridge is the key to pulling this salad together quickly.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/24/magazine/green-goddess-dressing-salad-jessica-battilana.html', 'snippet': 'Mayonnaise from your fridge is the key to pulling this salad together quickly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I have strong feelings about mayonnaise, the kinds of feelings that recently made me reconsider a friendship. Jessica Battilana has been my kindred cooking spirit for more than 10 years. Our careers as cooks and writers have taken us through the same Bay Area restaurants, bakeries, magazines and newspapers. We’ve explored year-round farmers’ markets and 12-course tasting menus. And through the fine-dining gantlet, we emerged as champions for no-nonsense, flavorful home cooking.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-eat3-copy/29mag-eat3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 422, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-eat3-copy/29mag-eat3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 422}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-eat3-copy/29mag-eat3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 720, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-eat3-copy/29mag-eat3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1440, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-e