jam yourself into the ground like a lawn dart,” says Austin Anderson, a trick rider and horse stuntman from Troup, Tex. You’ll have a few fractions of a second in the air before you land; use them to protect yourself by tucking your chin to your chest and preparing to roll, which will increase your deceleration time and distribute the force of the impact over more of your body.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-tip/29mag-29tip-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 620, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-tip/29mag-29tip-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 620}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-tip/29mag-29tip-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 991, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-tip/29mag-29tip-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1400, 'width': 1354, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-tip/29mag-tip-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-tip/29mag-tip-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-tip/29mag-tip-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Fall Off a Horse', 'kicker': 'Tip', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How to Fall Off A Horse', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horses', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stunts and Stuntmen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-25T09:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Malia Wollan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Malia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wollan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3db2f3e3-fdce-5ae5-a2c0-d2054e5b7d50', 'word_count': 392, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3db2f3e3-fdce-5ae5-a2c0-d2054e5b7d50'}, {'abstract': 'Selected by Terrance Hayes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/25/magazine/the-year-the-law-changed.html', 'snippet': 'Selected by Terrance Hayes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Note the way slant and full rhyming couplets provide graceful rails for this poem’s difficult subject. Carol Muske-Dukes glides associatively down the rhyme scheme until around Line 14: “When my name/was called.” The end rhyme dissolves — or is absorbed into the lines of the poem. Yes, the subject here is daring and tenderly drawn, maybe controversial. Poems are rarely simply about subject. They are almost always about poetry: form, sound and deliberations of order and disorder. Selected by Terrance Hayes', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-poem/29mag-29poem-t_CA0-articleLarge.png', 'height': 257, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/29/magazine/29mag-poem/29mag-29poem-t_CA0-articleLarge.png', 'xlarge