fore she let go. When I saw her a month later, she was still in physical pain from her injuries and emotional pain from having endured a traumatic experience.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/25/opinion/25burnett-zeigler/25burnett-zeigler-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/25/opinion/25burnett-zeigler/25burnett-zeigler-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/25/opinion/25burnett-zeigler/25burnett-zeigler-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/25/opinion/25burnett-zeigler/25burnett-zeigler-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/25/opinion/25burnett-zeigler/25burnett-zeigler-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/25/opinion/25burnett-zeigler/25burnett-zeigler-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/25/opinion/25burnett-zeigler/25burnett-zeigler-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Strong and Stressed Black Woman', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-25T09:45:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Inger E. Burnett-Zeigler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Inger', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Burnett-Zeigler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa04a63b-616e-591a-9554-f23aec31ddd9', 'word_count': 1012, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa04a63b-616e-591a-9554-f23aec31ddd9'}, {'abstract': 'An environmentalist and author tells Fixes how his organization rates the benefits of measures already in use to minimize climate change.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/25/opinion/a-smorgasbord-of-solutions-for-global-warming.html', 'snippet': 'An environmentalist and author tells Fixes how his organization rates the benefits of measures already in use to minimize climate change.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thinking about Earth Day, I did a Google search for “global warming solutions.” Up popped a slew of “what you can do” lists from leading scientific and environmental organizations. When I dug in, however, I found the suggestions rather general — “reduce emissions,” “stop deforestation,” “consume less,” “be efficient,” “eat smart.” The lists were also mainly about what we ought to do, rather than about what people are doing, where we are seeing progress and how we might build on those opportunities. I wondered: How do we translate these imperatives into action for people in different fields and positions?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/25/opinion/25BornsteinWEB1/25BornsteinW