ain drips blood-red from the rusted steel columns that hang from the ceiling, commemorating the thousands of lynchings of black Americans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/25/opinion/alabama-lynching-memorial.html', 'snippet': 'Rain drips blood-red from the rusted steel columns that hang from the ceiling, commemorating the thousands of lynchings of black Americans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From the bottom of a gentle grassy slope a hundred feet away, the rusted steel columns look like nothing remarkable. Lined up in rows under an open-air shed, they could be abandoned I-beams or castoffs from an old railroad bridge. Then, before you know it, you’re among them. Hundreds of reddish-brown hulls, each maybe six feet tall, surrounding you. Each bolted to a thick metal post anchored not to the floor but to the ceiling. Each — you imagine, feeling a bit crazy — watching.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/26/opinion/26thu1webF/26thu1webF-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/26/opinion/26thu1webF/26thu1webF-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/26/opinion/26thu1webF/26thu1webF-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/26/opinion/26thu1webF/26thu1webF-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/26/opinion/26thu1webF/26thu1webF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/26/opinion/26thu1webF/26thu1webF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/26/opinion/26thu1webF/26thu1webF-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At This Memorial, the Monuments Bleed', 'kicker': 'Editorial Observer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At This Memorial, the Monuments Bleed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Memorial for Peace and Justice (Montgomery, Ala)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lynching', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monuments and Memorials (Structures)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Equal Justice Initiative', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stevenson, Bryan A (1959- )', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Montgomery (Ala)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Southern States (US)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-25T15:52:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jesse Wegman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jesse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wegman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/62813c04-241c-506d-9ddf-089dca7ad2dc', 'word_count': 880, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62813c04-241c-506d-9ddf-089dca7ad2dc'}, {'abstract': 'A new memorial and museum in Montgomery, Ala., bring attention to a disturbing chapter of the nation’s history — one that in some ways lives on.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/25/opinion/american-lynchings-memorial.html', 'snippet': 'A new memorial and museum in Montgomery, Ala., bring attention to a disturbing chapter of the nation’s history — one that in some ways lives on.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The carnivals of death where African-American men, women and children were hanged, burned and dismembered as cheering crowds of whites looked on were the cornerstone of white supremacist rule in the Jim Crow-era South. These bloody spectacles terrified black communities into submission and showed whites that there would be no price to pay for murdering black people who asserted the right to vote, competed with whites in business — or so much as brushed against a white person on the sidewalk.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/26/opinion/26thu1weba/merlin_137233524_6556c300-b562-4d1f-88fe-db4d710747c6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/26/opinion/26thu1weba/merlin_137233524_6556c300-b562-4d1f-88fe-db4d710747c6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/26/opinion/26thu1weba/merlin_137233524_6556c300-b562-4d1f-88fe-db4d710747c6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/26/opinion/26thu1weba/merlin_137233524_6556c300-b562-4d1f-88fe-db4d710747c6-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo