'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Baker and Eileen Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Eileen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a4fce57-4fd6-5d8d-87f8-97fa444c2f10', 'word_count': 1536, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a4fce57-4fd6-5d8d-87f8-97fa444c2f10'}, {'abstract': 'Protests now sweeping Nicaragua represent a rejection of Daniel Ortega’s blatant aspiration to perpetuate himself and his family in power at any cost.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/26/opinion/daniel-ortega-nicaragua.html', 'snippet': 'Protests now sweeping Nicaragua represent a rejection of Daniel Ortega’s blatant aspiration to perpetuate himself and his family in power at any cost.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAMBRIDGE, Mass. — Forty years ago, on Jan. 10, 1978, my grandfather, Pedro Joaquín Chamorro, was shot and killed in Nicaragua. He was a journalist and civic leader who opposed the dynastic dictatorship of Nicaragua’s Somoza family. Scholars widely cite his assassination as the trigger of the regime’s popular overthrow. My grandfather has lived on as a national symbol of civil liberties — which is why both as his grandson and as a young historian of the Sandinista revolution, I feel I must speak out.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/26/opinion/26chamorro-nyt/merlin_137268678_a37f22d1-1a99-4bba-ba77-4513d7ce6be5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 375, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/26/opinion/26chamorro-nyt/merlin_137268678_a37f22d1-1a99-4bba-ba77-4513d7ce6be5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 375}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/26/opinion/26chamorro-nyt/merlin_137268678_a37f22d1-1a99-4bba-ba77-4513d7ce6be5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 640, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/26/opinion/26chamorro-nyt/merlin_137268678_a37f22d1-1a99-4bba-ba77-4513d7ce6be5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1280, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/26/opinion/26chamorro-nyt/26chamorro-nyt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/26/opinion/26chamorro-nyt/26chamorro-nyt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/26/opinion/26chamorro-nyt/26chamorro-nyt-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Beginning of the End for Ortega?', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Beginning of the End for Ortega?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nicaragua', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ortega, Daniel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sandinista National Liberation Front', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-26T14:30:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'sec