'pub_date': '2018-04-26T18:42:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jessica Valenti', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Valenti', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/970f4274-5ad8-5a4d-abb8-7ac6508e6a97', 'word_count': 929, 'uri': 'nyt://article/970f4274-5ad8-5a4d-abb8-7ac6508e6a97'}, {'abstract': 'Catalans have come together on the feast of Sant Jordi for centuries. This year, after months of secessionist turmoil, political division was perhaps inevitable.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/26/world/europe/catalonia-sant-jordi-independence-books-flowers.html', 'snippet': 'Catalans have come together on the feast of Sant Jordi for centuries. This year, after months of secessionist turmoil, political division was perhaps inevitable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since medieval times Catalans have celebrated the feast of their patron, Sant Jordi, or Saint George, on April 23, which happens to coincide with the deaths of both William Shakespeare and Miguel de Cervantes.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/26/world/europe/xxcatalonia1/merlin_137210184_e37eb869-c63a-4097-bbb4-b5fe97841c8e-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/26/world/europe/xxcatalonia1/merlin_137210184_e37eb869-c63a-4097-bbb4-b5fe97841c8e-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/26/world/europe/xxcatalonia1/merlin_137210184_e37eb869-c63a-4097-bbb4-b5fe97841c8e-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width