[{'firstname': 'Raphael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Minder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ab69018-34ec-52e9-b76a-cc46968d09c8', 'word_count': 971, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ab69018-34ec-52e9-b76a-cc46968d09c8'}, {'abstract': 'New York’s waters are home to 20 species of whales and dolphins. One man aims to count them all. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/26/nyregion/arthur-kopelman-works-the-other-hamptons.html', 'snippet': 'New York’s waters are home to 20 species of whales and dolphins. One man aims to count them all. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'Arthur Kopelman has been monitoring the local whale and seal populations for long enough that he knows some of the regulars by name. There’s the harbor seal with a marking like a hammer behind her right ear that he has followed for 13 years, so he named her Hammerhead Right. And another with a mark under her left front flipper that looks like a vessel out of Star Trek, so he named her Enterprise. ', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/17/nyregion/00ALBUM-WHALEWATCHING1/00ALBUM-WHALEWATCHING1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/17/nyregion/00ALBUM-WHALEWATCHING1/00ALBUM-WHALEWATCHING1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 750}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/17/nyregion/00ALBUM-WHALEWATCHING1/00ALBUM-WHALEWATCHING1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 819, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/17/nyregion/00ALBUM-