ue': 'Khan Younis (Gaza Strip)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-27T05:01:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fadi Abu Shammalah', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fadi', 'middlename': 'Abu', 'lastname': 'Shammalah', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/fde7fa6c-a5db-54c6-9bb0-a674972b660b', 'word_count': 1259, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fde7fa6c-a5db-54c6-9bb0-a674972b660b'}, {'abstract': 'In Italy’s recent elections, half of the voters chose Euroskeptic parties. But that doesn’t mean Italy will abandon the Treaty of Rome.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/27/opinion/italy-leave-european-union.html', 'snippet': 'In Italy’s recent elections, half of the voters chose Euroskeptic parties. But that doesn’t mean Italy will abandon the Treaty of Rome.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MILAN — Eight weeks after Italy’s election, it’s still possible that a government will be formed by the Five Star Movement and the League, two parties that have campaigned passionately against Brussels and the European Union in the past. In the last few days they have quarreled, but it seems sure that one of them, maybe both, will be the force driving Italy’s next government, with the question before it being whether to continue on that anti-European path.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/27/opinion/27Severgnini/merlin_119962514_9e8c1133-7d49-4c10-8e44-39ac29b9e562-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/27/opinion/27Severgnini/merlin_119962514_9e8c1133-7d49-4c10-8e44-39ac29b9e562-artic