 between the leaders of North Korea and South Korea, and declared that in his own planned talks with the North he would not be fooled the way he said his predecessors had been on dismantling its nuclear weapons program.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/28/us/politics/28dc-trumpkorea/merlin_137417094_476f77e1-0d1d-486b-95a4-c8efd42ef4d6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/28/us/politics/28dc-trumpkorea/merlin_137417094_476f77e1-0d1d-486b-95a4-c8efd42ef4d6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/28/us/politics/28dc-trumpkorea/merlin_137417094_476f77e1-0d1d-486b-95a4-c8efd42ef4d6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/28/us/politics/28dc-trumpkorea/merlin_137417094_476f77e1-0d1d-486b-95a4-c8efd42ef4d6-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/28/us/politics/28dc-trumpkorea/28dc-trumpkorea-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/28/us/politics/28dc-trumpkorea/28dc-trumpkorea-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/28/us/politics/28dc-trumpkorea/28dc-trumpkorea-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Trump, Hailing Korean Talks, Says He Won’t Be Hoodwinked by North', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kim Jong-un', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-04-27T18:12:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Washington', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Hirschfeld Davis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': 'Hirschfeld', 'lastname': 'Davis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc4874ba-4c46-5e9c-8638-a3c5d90be506', 'word_count': 456, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc4874ba-4c46-5e9c-8638-a3c5d90be506'}, {'abstract': 'Justin Strauss, who got his start at the underground Mudd Club, and the Black Madonna share playlists inspired by the era exclusively with T.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/27/t-magazine/80s-playlists-justin-strauss-black-madonna-dj.html', 'snippet': 'Justin Strauss, who got his start at the underground Mudd Club, and the Black Madonna share playlists inspired by the era exclusively with T.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In late 1979, when the D.J. Justin Strauss first brought a bag of records to spin at the Mudd Club, the downtown New York City bar that’s now the stuff of underground lore, the term “disc jockey” didn’t mean much yet — and it certainly didn’t evoke celebrity status. “It wasn’t anything that anyone thought about — I didn’t think about it,” Strauss says now. “I didn’t wake up and go, ‘I want to be a D.J.’ It just sort of happened naturally.” He had left New York, his hometown, to live in Los Angeles with his power-pop group Milk ’n’ Cookies. The band eventually split and his then-girlfriend persuaded him to come back to the city. “She said, ‘You gotta come. This club opened up and you’d be great to play your records there,’” he remembers. This was the era of the slick, moneyed glamour of Studio 54, and the Mudd Club was, in many ways, a reaction to it. “There were no rules — you could play a rockabilly song next to a rap song or a reggae song or the Buzzcocks or whatever it was — it all made sense and everyone danced,” he said. As for the crowd, it “was just a mix of downtown kids, artists … Keith Haring, Jean-Michel [Basquiat].” Strauss spent the rest of the decade spinning at big — and now shuttered — clubs like Area, the Ritz, Tunnel and Limelight. He ultimately began remixing and producing original tracks; he still D.J.s around the world and has a show on the Lot Radio, based in Brooklyn.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/30/t-magazine/26tmag-djs01/26tmag-djs01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/30/t-magazine/26tmag-djs01/26tmag-djs01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/30/t-magazine/26tmag-djs01/26tmag-djs01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/30/t-magazine/26tmag-djs01/26tmag-djs01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/30/t-magazine/26tmag-djs01/26tmag-djs01-thumbStandard.jpg