, 'news_desk': 'Washington', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Landler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Landler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/391314a0-b745-540a-854a-4c9c04a89407', 'word_count': 1205, 'uri': 'nyt://article/391314a0-b745-540a-854a-4c9c04a89407'}, {'abstract': 'The two characters were reunited, in a sense, in Episode 2 of the new season. Their history? It’s complicated.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/30/watching/westworld-season-2-episode-2-william-dolores-reunion.html', 'snippet': 'The two characters were reunited, in a sense, in Episode 2 of the new season. Their history? It’s complicated.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Coming just two episodes into a season that was nearly 17 months in the making, Dolores and William’s reunion in this week’s episode of “Westworld” was bound to prompt some questions. For starters, is fair to call it a reunion when it happens in flashbacks? More important: What, exactly, was their deal again?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/30/watching/30westworld-dolores/30westworld-dolores-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/30/watching/30westworld-dolores/30westworld-dolores-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/30/watching/30westworld-dolores/30westworld-dolores-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url':